{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-87731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}-3\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}-3 & \\le 1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4} & \\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ -(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & \\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1708f7b3c9834eae8623757d2626eea8912d2290198513f4322bb46d444e0dd"} {"question" "q-87731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+4"] ["math" "x\\le -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-4"] ["math" "x\\le -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ${12}^{x}$ is always positive and $-{2}^{6x-1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1708f7b3c9834eae8623757d2626eea8912d2290198513f4322bb46d444e0dd"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}"] ["text" "all real numbers"] ["text" "no solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-87862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "all real numbers"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}+1<-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}+1 & <-5 \\\\ 2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7} & <-6 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7} & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $\\frac{1}{3}>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}$ cannot be less than $-3.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}<-3\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1708f7b3c9834eae8623757d2626eea8912d2290198513f4322bb46d444e0dd"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<-3"] ["math" "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}+6"] ["math" "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}-1"] ["text" "all real numbers"] ["text" "no solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-115233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "no solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $-(0.75)^{2-3x}<(0.98)^{3x-1}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(0.98)^{3x-1}$ is always positive and $-(0.75)^{2-3x}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-(0.75)^{2-3x}<(0.98)^{3x-1}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1708f7b3c9834eae8623757d2626eea8912d2290198513f4322bb46d444e0dd"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-277887" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-365299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}\\le 3\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-365299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 9\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-365299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1708f7b3c9834eae8623757d2626eea8912d2290198513f4322bb46d444e0dd"} {"question" "q-365299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067615/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4476d1866b45e23b38d74104b172b9636c6f4817e9ab1c11b7f4295184c211c"} {"question" "q-365299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡